    },
    {
      "id": "60tQIKie3YYi7gmwcrTlX",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 404.7663273917101,
      "y": 1885.0000001111111,
      "width": 798.3595581054688,
      "height": 450,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#343a40",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b16",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 643503997,
      "version": 1379,
      "versionNonce": 1141078301,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1795697921622,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "A block goes to an SM only when all four pools have room.\nBlocks that fit = the smallest of the four. That pool is the limiter.\n\nShared memory saves global reads, but it is a pool tool.\nUse too much per block and fewer blocks fit, so occupancy drops.\n\nOur tirled matmul uses little (8 B per thread), so shared memory is not its limiter.\nA kernel with big tiles can hit it.\n\nShared memory per SM differs between cards. The same kernel can be fine on\none and limited on another.\n\nThe book's fix: size shared memory at launch, not in code, meaning the host\ndecides the size when it launches the kernel, not in the kernel itself.\n\nRule of thumb: on-chip memory raises the FLOP/B ratio, meaning\nthe kernel becomes less memory bound,\nbut every byte per block costs occupancy. Balance the two. ",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "center",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "A block goes to an SM only when all four pools have room.\nBlocks that fit = the smallest of the four. That pool is the limiter.\n\nShared memory saves global reads, but it is a pool tool.\nUse too much per block and fewer blocks fit, so occupancy drops.\n\nOur tirled matmul uses little (8 B per thread), so shared memory is not its limiter.\nA kernel with big tiles can hit it.\n\nShared memory per SM differs between cards. The same kernel can be fine on\none and limited on another.\n\nThe book's fix: size shared memory at launch, not in code, meaning the host\ndecides the size when it launches the kernel, not in the kernel itself.\n\nRule of thumb: on-chip memory raises the FLOP/B ratio, meaning\nthe kernel becomes less memory bound,\nbut every byte per block costs occupancy. Balance the two. ",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    }
  ],
